Compliance Posture Summary
The compliance pillar scores at 76 with three warnings, all of them converging on the same structural absence: no sensitivity labels exist in this tenant. Because a sensitivity label is the only instruction Copilot can read to understand how content should be handled, that absence means Copilot currently operates without any classification signal — it cannot distinguish content that warrants caution from content that does not. Without labels configured, neither manual classification by users nor automatic classification by policy is possible, so the gap is not one of adoption lag but of missing infrastructure.

This report does not grade your tenant against GDPR, SOX, HIPAA, FINRA or any other regulatory framework, and the section a compliance review would normally use to do that is deliberately absent. No check this assessment runs maps a Microsoft 365 control to a regulatory requirement, classifies your content as personal, health or financial data, or reads your audit-log retention period. The figures below are measurements of your tenant's configuration; a framework alignment verdict would be a legal judgement, and this assessment has not made one. Nothing here should be read as saying your obligations under any framework are met, or that they are not — only that this scan did not evaluate them.

Data Lifecycle & Records Management
The governance score of 56 reflects two findings that share a root cause: groups exist with no owner, and public Teams are visible and joinable without one. Without an accountable owner, there is no one to decide when a group or Team has outlived its purpose — which means content inside those spaces accumulates indefinitely rather than being reviewed, archived, or deleted.

That accumulation problem connects directly to the compliance findings. No sensitivity labels are configured, none have been adopted, and no automatic classification is in place. Without labels, there is no mechanism to distinguish content that should be retained from content that should be disposed of, because retention and classification decisions typically depend on each other — you cannot reliably act on content you have not categorised.

The practical consequence for Copilot is straightforward: content that nothing prompts anyone to review or dispose of stays in place, and Copilot treats what it can reach as current. In this tenant, the absence of labels and the absence of accountable owners compound each other — stale content in ungoverned spaces carries no signal that would cause it to age out of Copilot's view.

