Copilot Readiness Impact
The tenant's Copilot readiness score sits at 53% against a gate of 82 — a gap of 29 points — and the compliance posture is a direct structural contributor to why that gap exists. Copilot does not infer sensitivity; it reads a label as an instruction and treats unlabelled content as unrestricted. With no sensitivity labels configured anywhere in this tenant, every piece of content Copilot can reach is, from its perspective, unclassified and fair game — there is nothing for it to act on, restrict, or surface selectively.

That matters more acutely because of the standing-access condition already recorded: 1 of 93 scanned SharePoint sites is shared with Everyone except external users. Copilot inherits permissions rather than intent, so any internal user can already reach that site's content through normal access — and Copilot will surface it through prompts with equal indifference, because there is no label present to signal otherwise.

Sitting underneath both of these is the critical finding that no Microsoft 365 Copilot license has been found in this tenant. That is the prerequisite condition — without it, none of the downstream label-enforcement or access-scoping behaviour is in play at all, which is the primary reason the readiness score is where it is.

